WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:04.265
An internet provider records how many network outages it has each

00:04.365 --> 00:08.428
day. Most days none, some days one, occasionally two or three.

00:08.528 --> 00:12.189
How many outages should it expect per day? And what does

00:12.289 --> 00:16.017
'expect' even mean, when the answer isn't a whole number?

00:18.116 --> 00:22.639
A discrete random variable is described by a list of values and

00:22.739 --> 00:27.995
their probabilities. Two numbers summarise it: its mean and its variance.

00:29.083 --> 00:32.428
Here is the probability function: half of all days have no

00:32.528 --> 00:36.764
outage, thirty percent have one, fifteen percent two, five percent three.

00:36.864 --> 00:38.962
The bars add up to one, as they must.

00:39.050 --> 00:42.841
Think of each bar as a weight sitting at its value.

00:42.941 --> 00:47.342
The expected value is the balance point: zero times a half,

00:47.442 --> 00:51.767
plus one times point three, plus two times point one five,

00:51.867 --> 00:56.269
plus three times point zero five. Point seven five outages.

00:57.366 --> 01:00.902
Point seven five outages can't happen on any single day.

01:01.002 --> 01:04.084
So what does it mean? Watch the average number of

01:04.184 --> 01:06.357
outages per day, as the days go by.

01:06.450 --> 01:09.710
It jumps around at first, then settles down to point seven

01:09.810 --> 01:13.419
five. The expected value is the long-run average: what you'd get

01:13.519 --> 01:15.910
per day, on average, over a very long time.

01:17.000 --> 01:20.568
The mean tells us the centre. For the spread, we do what

01:20.668 --> 01:25.088
we did with data: measure each value's distance from the mean, square

01:25.188 --> 01:29.673
it, and average. But now the average is weighted by the probabilities.

01:29.766 --> 01:33.443
The rare day with three outages sits far from the mean, so it

01:33.543 --> 01:37.530
has a big square, but it only counts with weight five percent. The

01:37.630 --> 01:42.236
weighted average of the squares is the variance, about point seven nine, and

01:42.336 --> 01:46.385
its square root, about point eight nine, is the standard deviation.

01:48.483 --> 01:51.523
Last idea. Suppose the regulator fines the provider

01:51.623 --> 01:54.109
two hundred times the square of the number

01:54.209 --> 01:57.003
of outages, each day. What's the expected fine?

01:57.100 --> 02:01.647
The tempting shortcut is to plug in the average day: two hundred times

02:01.747 --> 02:06.892
point seven five squared, one hundred and twelve manat. That's wrong. The right

02:06.992 --> 02:11.141
way is to apply the fine to every possible day and then average:

02:11.241 --> 02:16.054
two hundred and seventy manat. Because the fine grows with the square, the

02:16.154 --> 02:21.166
bad days cost disproportionately more. In general, the expected value of g of

02:21.266 --> 02:23.357
Y is not g of the expected value.

02:25.450 --> 02:28.637
So: the expected value is both the balance point of the

02:28.737 --> 02:33.597
probability bars and the long-run average. The variance is the probability-weighted

02:33.697 --> 02:37.542
average square. And to find the expectation of a function, average

02:37.642 --> 02:41.127
the function over the values; don't apply it to the average.
